00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:06.360
Let's jump into it.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.280
So here is how it
actually works.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.360
Nothing changes for you.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.730
You continue using
existing clients

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.920
and standard SQL
to issue queries.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.550
These queries flow through the
SQL frontend, and they're parsed

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:19.470
and optimized, then
they're pushed

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:22.740
into the distribution
engine, and that's broken

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.740
down into different plans that
are executed by the system.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.210
The difference is
under the hood.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:32.460
Instead of relying only on CPUs,
queries or portions of queries,

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.650
can be pushed down to GPUs.

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.150
And this allows each backend

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:40.740
to execute complex operations
much more efficiently

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.030
on these GPUs.

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:46.050
Now, all of this runs in
parallel across nodes and GPUs,

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:50.010
which results in a combined
final output and an output

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:53.550
that your users receive
faster than ever before.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:57.300
From a user's perspective, it's
the same SQL surface area.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.520
It's the same query
that you're writing.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.960
And it's leveraging the same
exact architecture with GPUs

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:09.840
on top of it to power your
users with faster insights.

