00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:57.090
Now, first one, this one's a
bit of a "finally" moment.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:59.760
We all know customers have
been waiting for this

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.070
for quite some time, because
that's let's be honest,

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:04.800
ALTER COLUMN is pretty
much table stakes.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.790
Up until now, making data type
changes meant rebuilding tables.

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:12.000
Now you can just alter columns
in place, no rewrites,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:13.830
no disruption, no drama.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:16.590
It just works.

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:19.260
ALTER TABLE inside
a transaction.

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:21.660
Here we're bringing
transactional safety

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:23.070
to schema changes.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:26.700
Today when a customer makes
multiple changes to a table,

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:30.180
there's always a risk that
something fails halfway through,

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:33.030
leaving things in an
inconsistent state.

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:34.410
That's painful to fix.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:35.940
With explicit transactions,

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:38.880
you can group multiple ALTER
TABLE operations together

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:42.300
as a single unit, so
everything either succeeds

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:43.710
or nothing succeeds.

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.060
If something goes wrong,

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.400
all changes are rolled
back automatically,

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:49.080
which means no partial updates

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:51.600
and no clean-up work
after the fact.

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.040
The result is a much safer, more
predictable schema evolution,

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:59.520
especially for those
more complex changes.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.850
T-SQL in the Fabric warehouse
continues to evolve

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:06.330
at a rapid pace, with recent
updates introducing fuzzy

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:09.270
string-matching capabilities
based on Levenshtein

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:13.800
and Jaro-Winkler algorithms, new
string concatenation operations,

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:17.010
additional functions like
UNISTR, DATE_BUCKET,

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.800
along with new aggregates
like ANY_VALUE

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:22.590
that expand expressiveness
and analytical power

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:25.020
of the Fabric Warehouse.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:28.200
Scalar UDFs in Fabric Data
Warehouse are evolving

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:31.200
to support richer, more
complex business logic,

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:35.610
bringing procedural constructs
like loops, multipath execution,

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:37.590
and complex
if/then/else branches

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:39.510
into your SQL workflows.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.640
These are built for
analytical scenarios

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.320
and optimized for scale.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:46.440
They allow you to write
cleaner, reusable logic

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:49.330
without compromising
performance.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:54.690
Super excited about this one,
lakehouse table health checks.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:58.290
One of the biggest challenges
that customers report to us is

00:12:58.290 --> 00:13:00.480
that when they're using
a SQL analytics endpoint

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:03.570
to query their lakehouse data,

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:05.970
their data in their
lakehouse isn't always

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:07.290
in an optimal state.

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:09.780
It often requires
manual maintenance.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:13.530
Over time things like small
files, fragmentations,

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:15.960
excessive updates and
deletes build up,

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:18.840
and then directly impacts
query performance.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:21.720
The problem is when
performance slows down,

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:24.990
it's pretty often hard to
pinpoint why it happened.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:28.230
It turns into a vague
"something's wrong" situation,

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:30.210
but it's kind of
hard to know why.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:32.640
What we're doing here is
we're making it a lot easier

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.710
for you to self-diagnose.

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:38.910
We now have an official
customer-facing diagnostic tool.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:40.650
It's a simple store
procedure called

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:42.720
"spgettablehealthmetrics".

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:45.300
With a single command, you
can quickly check the health

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:48.720
of your tables and understand
exactly what went wrong.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:51.690
It doesn't just tell you
that performance is off,

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:54.120
it actually pinpoints
the root cause.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.870
Whether it's fragmentation,
small files, too many deletes

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.420
or delayed checkpoints,
you'd get very clear

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.310
and actionable insights.

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:05.460
So instead of guessing
or manually inspecting,

00:14:05.460 --> 00:14:08.280
you immediately know
what needs to be fixed.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.200
And it isn't just reactive.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:12.930
You can plug this
into your pipelines.

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:16.260
So table health is
continuously monitored,

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:19.260
and maintenance can happen
proactively at scale.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:21.150
The key idea here is simple,

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:23.550
we're tuning performance
feels slow --

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:26.370
or rather we're turning
performance feels slow

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:29.340
into actionable intelligence,
making it much easier

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:33.790
to keep your data optimized and
your queries running super-fast.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:37.380
Now, everybody that's familiar

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:39.180
with the Fabric Data
Warehouse knows

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:43.710
that our out-of-the-box
retention policy is 30 days.

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:45.990
And this doesn't
suit all workloads.

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:48.840
Some customers want a
higher retention period,

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:51.150
other customers want a
lower retention period,

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:54.120
depending on what
their workloads need.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:56.700
Retention allows
you to time travel,

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:59.520
do point-in-time restores,
perform table clones

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:03.240
for the data retention history
period that you specify.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.580
So today we're announcing
the ability to modify

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:08.760
that default configurable
retention setting anywhere

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:11.490
from one to 120 days.

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:14.850
This flexibility allows teams
to balance operational needs

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:17.100
with storage cost optimization,

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:19.530
ensuring historical
data is retained

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:21.690
for only as long as needed.

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:24.300
Now, this feature is
a super-exciting one.

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:26.580
It's a real gamechanger
for performance.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:30.000
I want to introduce today to
you distributive bitmap filters

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.370
and how they help
speed up queries.

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:35.130
At a high level, the
idea is pretty simply.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:38.280
We run JOINED especially
between large fact tables

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:40.320
and a smaller dimension table,

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:43.470
a lot of reasons the fact table
don't actually match anything

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:45.270
useful on the other side.

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:47.580
So we end up scanning
and moving a lot of data

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:49.260
that we just don't need.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:51.030
Distributed Bitmap Filters solve

00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:53.100
that problem early
in the process.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:57.270
We take the smaller table,
build a compact filter from it,

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:59.280
and then push that
filter down to

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.770
where the large table
is being scanned.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.470
That way, we can
quickly eliminate rows

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:06.870
that will never match before
we even do the drawing.

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:08.850
How awesome is that?

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:11.910
The benefit is that we reduce
how much data we need to move

00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:14.910
around the system, and everybody
knows that's really expensive,

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:15.510
right?

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.970
So less IO, fewer
resource requests,

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:20.580
and overall faster
query execution.

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:24.360
This is particularly helpful
for selective JOINs.

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:27.720
But it also improves performance
for GROUP BYs aggregations

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:30.480
where filtering early
makes a huge difference.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.100
So if you look at the diagram,

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:34.020
the green represents
the rows we keep

00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:36.750
and the red are the rows
we would normally process

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.460
but can now skip.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.640
The key takeaway is that we're
doing less work up front,

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:44.460
which leads to faster
queries end to end.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:46.860
This feature is coming
soon and we expect it

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:49.470
to drive pretty significant
performance gains

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:51.540
to common warehouse workloads.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:54.870
Beyond scale, we've also
reimagined the day-to-day

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:59.340
developer experiences in the
SQL editor the small papercuts

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:02.310
that add up over a
workday, and we've gone

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:04.560
after them pretty deliberately.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.470
First, the new table
overview page.

00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:10.470
Today, when developers want
to understand a table,

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:14.040
they end up writing throwaway
queries like, "Select top 100,"

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.990
just to get a feel for the data.

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:18.600
We're adding a rich
landing experience,

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:20.520
which gives you
everything in one place,

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:23.220
full-column metadata
with data types.

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:27.270
Second, we've streamlined
query management end to end.

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:31.800
We've add autosave controls,
copy queries, import and export

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:34.140
of SQL files, and bulk actions

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:36.510
to manage multiple
queries at once.

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:39.300
Whether they're interactive
on a single complex query

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:43.470
or maintaining a library of
dozens, the editor now keeps

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:46.050
up with how developers
actually work.

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:49.560
Third -- and this is the one
we're most excited about

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:51.450
and I'm sure you
will be as well,

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:56.280
Copilot is now embedded directly
in line in the SQL editor.

00:17:56.280 --> 00:18:00.120
So if you're stuck on a JOIN,
you want to optimize a query,

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:03.360
or need to translate a
business question into SQL,

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:06.360
Copilot is right
there in your flow.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:08.550
This is where AI
assistance starts to feel

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:12.720
like a true pair program
rather than a built-on tool.

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:15.120
The next pillar of this
release is integration

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:17.640
with broader Fabric ecosystem.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:20.910
We've deeply connected the
warehouse experience to the rest

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:23.400
of Fabric, which
is super exciting.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:26.300
Start with cross-engine
analytics.

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:29.900
We've integrated event house
endpoint and notebooks directly

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:31.910
into the warehouse experience.

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:34.860
That means you can
run KQL queries

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:38.340
against your warehouse data,
but jumping to a Spark Notebook

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:42.030
for heavier transformations
and to add assigned workflows,

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:44.700
all from the same editor.

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:48.630
Next, copy job creation is
now integrated directly

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:50.370
into the editor.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:53.790
Now you can kick off ingestion
and data movement jobs right

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.670
from where you're
already working.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:19:00.480
On the modeling side, Power BI
integration now includes Direct

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:03.120
Lake over OneLake
semantic model creation,

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:04.650
right from the warehouse.

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:08.970
You can go from raw tables to a
production-ready semantic model

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:11.370
in significantly fewer clicks.

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:15.110
And finally, we're adding
support for Fabric ontologies,

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:17.120
letting you define
business concepts,

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:20.540
hierarchies in relationships
over your warehouse tables.

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:24.440
This turns raw schema into a
shared business vocabulary,

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:26.840
which becomes
especially powerful

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:30.290
as AI agents start querying
your data and need

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:32.630
to understand what
things actually mean,

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:34.910
not just what columns exist.

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:38.160
From a single editor,
you can analyze, model,

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:42.040
and operationalize your
data without breaking flow.

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:45.000
Next up, we have some

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:47.760
of the awesome new enterprise
readiness features,

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:49.710
which Rakesh is going to cover.

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:52.020
Over to you, Rakesh.

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:53.220
RAKESH KRISHNAN:
Thank you, Joanna.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:55.350
I'm really excited to
talk about a number

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:58.140
of exciting enterprise
readiness features.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:02.090
So first up, one of the most
consistent feedback we have

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:05.660
hired from our user community is
the experience didn't quite hold

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:08.150
up when you loaded
a large warehouse

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:09.770
with thousands of tables.

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:12.140
The object explorer
felt a little sluggish.

00:20:12.140 --> 00:20:14.250
Expanding nodes took forever.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:16.470
So we've been hard at
work at addressing this,

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:19.110
and we reengineered it
from the ground up,

00:20:19.110 --> 00:20:20.970
built specifically for scale.

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:24.300
The new updated explorer
loads really fast

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:28.050
and stays responsive even
on the largest warehouses,

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:29.970
(inaudible) testing that
the warehouse containing

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:31.530
over 10,000 objects.

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:34.890
With thousands of tables,
views and stored procedures,

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:38.850
we saw almost a 2x improvement
in (inaudible) performance.

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:41.010
But we didn't stop at
just the explorer.

00:20:41.010 --> 00:20:44.160
We also re-abled the results
grid from the ground up.

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.470
White tables with many columns

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:50.070
and full resources now
render in a jiffy.

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:53.130
We added native support
for large object types.

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:57.090
So working with
(inaudible) binary,

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:59.070
along with column resizing,

00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:02.580
you will see a substantially
improved rendering performance.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:04.350
And finally, we are rolling

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:07.320
out an enhanced intelligence
version as well.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:11.310
This means faster suggestions,
better context, and awareness

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:14.760
for your schema, and overall
improved reliability

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:16.970
for your warehouse experiences.

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:19.070
Now, this is the feature many

00:21:19.070 --> 00:21:21.770
of our customers have been
eagerly awaiting for,

00:21:21.770 --> 00:21:24.590
and the one I'm
most excited about.

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:27.230
I'm going to be talking about
a set of exciting changes

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:29.840
that are coming to the
SQL analytics endpoint.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:31.620
So first up, we are excited

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:35.280
to announce the public
preview of the metadata sync.

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:38.070
As you know, for every
lakehouse in Fabric,

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:40.560
there is a SQL analytics
endpoint created

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.090
that lets you query the data

00:21:42.090 --> 00:21:45.180
in Lakehouse using
familiar SQL constructs.

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:48.570
Now, historically once the
data lands in Lakehouse,

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:51.270
there's been a delay before
which you can query.

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:53.640
We've been working
hard on a new version

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.410
of the metadata sync built

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:57.660
on a completely
different architecture

00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:00.300
that (inaudible) increases
the sync performance,

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:02.640
minimizing data staleness.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:05.850
Your data, once it lands on the
lakehouse, will be available

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:08.670
in seconds, not
minutes for query.

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:12.090
We can't wait for you to
try this new capability.

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:15.180
So continuing on SQL analytics
endpoint improvements,

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.550
we're extending one of the
most loud capabilities

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:21.690
in the warehouse, time-travel
to SQL analytics endpoint.

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:25.860
Now, you can create any data on
SQL analytics endpoint exactly

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.380
as it looked at any
point in the first.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.830
The use cases are the ones that
every data team runs into day

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:35.250
to day, audits where you need
to prove that numbers --

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:37.650
what the numbers were
on a specific date,

00:22:37.650 --> 00:22:43.380
pointing to comparisons to see
how a metric drifted over time.

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.720
(Inaudible) when a downstream
report looks wrong and you need

00:22:45.720 --> 00:22:48.300
to create it back, all of this

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:51.240
with this feature it
eliminates the need for you

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:54.480
to create a snapshot or
create a copy of your data

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:56.220
for such critical scenarios.

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:58.800
Now, how far back your
data can go is governed

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:02.670
by the return string period that
you configure in your lakehouse.

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:05.880
Rounding out the SQL analytics
endpoint story for this build,

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:09.690
we are bringing enterprise-grade
CI/CD support as well.

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:12.540
This pattern customers have
asked for is straightforward.

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:15.570
Treat the CI/CD endpoint
like any other piece

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:18.210
of production infrastructure
with version control,

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:20.790
code review, and
automated deployments.

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:24.120
Today, that end-to-end
experience is suboptimal,

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:25.800
and we are closing that gap.

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:27.900
So first you have a
git-based version control

00:23:27.900 --> 00:23:30.180
for SQL endpoint schema,

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:32.940
represented as DacFX
database project.

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:34.830
Your schema lives in
your source control,

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:36.510
every change is reviewable,

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:39.090
and your endpoint becomes
a version artifact,

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:40.290
like any other.

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.720
Second, the integration
with Fabric pipelines

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:46.140
for deploying schema
changes makes it --

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:48.900
the deployments from
dev to test to prod

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:50.850
with minimal manual steps.

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:53.250
And finally, it is
natively integrated

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:55.830
with the Fabric
CI/CD experience,

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:59.850
and that ensures familiar
database DevOps tooling for --

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:02.550
that the enterprise
teams already use.

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:06.060
Now, continuing on the theme of
the CI/CD, I'm super excited

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:08.130
about this new announcement.

00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:12.060
We are adding support for pre-
and post- deployments scripts.

00:24:12.060 --> 00:24:14.100
When it comes to
enterprise CI/CD,

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:17.280
there is prep work you have to
do, checking prerequisites,

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:19.080
validating environments,

00:24:19.080 --> 00:24:21.300
making sure everything
is in the right state.

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:24.330
And there is cleanup afterwards:
applying permissions,

00:24:24.330 --> 00:24:27.420
configuring settings,
running validation checks.

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:31.920
Today, all of those deployments
live outside the deployment

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:34.050
cycle and in manual steps.

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:36.420
That's where many
releases break.

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:39.000
With the support for pre- and
post- deployment scripts,

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:41.220
we're addressing
these gaps directly.

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:44.550
This turns warehouse deployments
into something repeatable

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:48.600
and low risk, with
minimal manual steps.

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:51.150
The full release, the
prep, the schema chain,

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:53.910
the cleanup become
-- everything ensure

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.640
that this becomes one
governed artifact.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:25:04.470
And these two CI/CD changes are
coming soon to all your regions.

00:25:04.470 --> 00:25:08.940
And shifting gears quite a bit,
one of our biggest priorities is

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:10.830
to enable Enterprise customers

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:13.560
in helping modernize
their data stacks.

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:16.410
So getting customers from
their existing data warehouse,

00:25:16.410 --> 00:25:19.410
all the Fabric is one of
our key priorities for us,

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:22.380
and the migration assistant
is now generally available,

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:23.640
for a set of enhancements

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:26.730
that will make this journey
meaningfully simpler.

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:30.960
First, the live connectivity
is now generally available.

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:34.140
The system now connects
directly to your source system,

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:37.440
eliminating the need
for DACPAC extraction

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:39.300
or any manual prep work.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:42.930
This removes one of the most
common blockers customers are

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:45.630
running into today when
it comes to migration.

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:49.710
Another exciting announcement we
are having is that we have --

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:52.860
we now support.SQL
files as an input,

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:55.080
which is going into
public preview.

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:58.710
This is when you have existing
script schema definitions

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:00.060
or workloads you want to bring

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:03.510
across without going
to a live connection.

00:26:03.510 --> 00:26:06.690
Essentially, we support a
broad variety of sources now,

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:08.970
whether it's Azure
Synapse, SQL Server,

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:11.640
or effectively any T-SQL
database as a source.

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:13.500
The same tool covers most

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.720
of your enterprise
migration scenarios.

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:17.820
The bigger picture
is the migration

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:20.280
to Fabric has gotten much easier

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:22.530
with significantly
lower barriers.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:25.410
We are excited for you to
try all these features out

00:26:25.410 --> 00:26:28.560
and begin your Fabric journey.

00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:31.980
Now, the set of enterprise
features don't end there.

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:34.830
There are some exciting workload
management features that have --

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:36.450
that we are about to announce.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:40.980
I'm handing it back to
Joanna to talk about them.

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:43.020
JOANNA PODGOETSKY: Thank
you so much, Rakesh.

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:45.210
So in the Fabric Data Warehouse,

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:47.640
we've had intelligent
workload management built

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:49.140
in from the get-go.

00:26:49.140 --> 00:26:51.840
The system automatically
routes queries based

00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:52.920
on what they're doing.

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:54.870
Whether it's running
a select query

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:59.400
for your reporting workloads, or
ingesting and transforming data,

00:26:59.400 --> 00:27:02.460
different workloads don't
interfere with one another.

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:06.540
Be default, resources are
split evenly between select

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:08.370
and non-select workloads.

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:10.830
But in reality, some
environments are heavy

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:13.230
on reporting whilst
others are more focused

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:15.150
on ingestion and ETL.

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:18.420
With custom SQL pools, you
now have direct control

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:22.080
over that 50/50 split, so you
can allocate resources in a way

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:25.200
that better matches how your
workloads actually behave.

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:28.200
So for example, if
you want to have

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:31.290
that split more skewed towards
your reporting workloads,

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:34.530
you can increase the select
workload group to 80%,

00:27:34.530 --> 00:27:37.380
maybe you only need 20%
left over for ingestion.

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:39.360
It's all up to you.

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:41.220
We're evolving our billing model

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:45.690
to more closely match how real
data warehouse workloads behave.

00:27:45.690 --> 00:27:48.780
We will be moving to a
node-based billing model,

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:51.420
so you're billed on the
actual compute used

00:27:51.420 --> 00:27:52.920
to run your queries.

00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:56.040
Billing starts with a one-minute
minimum, and then moves

00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:59.730
to a per second granularity,
which means the larger

00:27:59.730 --> 00:28:02.190
and more stable
your workloads are,

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:04.740
the more that initial
minute gets monetized.

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:06.270
In practice, for the kinds

00:28:06.270 --> 00:28:08.760
of long-running
analytical queries common

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:11.400
in data warehousing
workloads, this results

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:15.450
in much more predictable and
efficient usage over time.

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:18.780
Combined with improvements
to our computer efficiency,

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:21.000
this model is designed
to better align

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.610
with steady
production-grade workloads.

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:26.790
And importantly, this
is just the foundation.

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:29.850
We'll continue to build more
efficiencies into the engine,

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:32.910
so over time, your
workloads benefit even more

00:28:32.910 --> 00:28:35.820
from this model without
any changes on your end.

00:28:35.820 --> 00:28:38.460
So as your workloads
scale and run longer,

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:42.310
this model increasingly
works in your favor.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:45.870
And let's talk about
cache cooldown.

00:28:45.870 --> 00:28:47.940
Cache cooldown gives you control

00:28:47.940 --> 00:28:51.510
over how long your compute
stays warm between queries.

00:28:51.510 --> 00:28:53.790
By configuring the
cooldown window,

00:28:53.790 --> 00:28:55.320
you can avoid cold starts

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.870
and keep frequently-accessed
workloads running

00:28:57.870 --> 00:28:59.460
with low latency.

00:28:59.460 --> 00:29:02.250
It also lets you balance
performance and cost,

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:05.790
especially for bursty or
intermittent usage patterns.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:09.930
And because scaling remains
automatic, the system can scale

00:29:09.930 --> 00:29:13.920
up or down, based on demand,
without manual intervention.

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:16.320
In short, you keep the
performance benefits

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.930
of warm queries while
staying efficient

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:20.760
when workloads are idle.

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:23.430
Really, really exciting stuff.

00:29:23.430 --> 00:29:27.720
All right, so if you're
excited about getting started,

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:30.300
we've made that
really easy for you.

00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:33.450
We've pulled together everything
you need in one place,

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:36.330
documentation to go
deeper, a free trial

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:39.270
so you can jump straight
in, and guided learning

00:29:39.270 --> 00:29:41.430
if you want a structured path.

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:44.220
If you're more technical,
we've got architecture content

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:46.860
to help you understand what's
happening under the hood.

00:29:46.860 --> 00:29:48.450
And if you want to stay ahead,

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:51.180
you can track what's coming
next on the roadmap.

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:52.980
And of course, if you
want the highlights

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:55.410
from everything we've
just discussed,

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:57.900
we've got the Build
roundups ready to go.

00:29:57.900 --> 00:30:01.350
So whether you are just starting
out or already deep in Fabric,

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:04.380
there's something here to
help you take the next step.

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:05.280
Thank you so much.

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:07.630
We hope you enjoyed
this session.
